241: ## Agent Orchestration Patterns
242: 
243: ### Sequential Chaining
244: Use when tasks have dependencies:
245: ```bash
246: # Planning → Implementation → Testing → Review
247: /ck:plan "implement user dashboard"
248: # For large refactors:
249: /ck:plan --deep "untangle the dashboard data flow"
250: /ck:plan --tdd "refactor dashboard state safely"
251: # After planning, use the exact absolute-path handoff command emitted by plan:
252: /ck:cook /absolute/path/to/plans/YYMMDD-HHMM-dashboard/plan.md
253: # If planning used --tdd, preserve it on handoff:
254: /ck:cook /absolute/path/to/plans/YYMMDD-HHMM-dashboard/plan.md --tdd
255: # Cook already runs testing and code review as part of the workflow.
256: 
257: # Alternative: Use /ck:cook for standalone implementation (plans internally)
258: /ck:cook "implement user dashboard"
259: /ck:cook "refactor dashboard state" --tdd
260: ```
261: 
262: ### Parallel Execution
263: Use for independent tasks:
264: ```bash
265: # Multiple researchers exploring different approaches
266: planner agent spawns:
267: - researcher (database options)
268: - researcher (authentication methods)
269: - researcher (UI frameworks)
270: # All report back to planner simultaneously
271: ```
272: 
273: ### Context Management
274: - Agents communicate through file system reports
275: - Context is preserved between agent handoffs
276: - Fresh context prevents conversation degradation
277: - Essential information is documented in markdown
