432: ## Model Context Protocol (MCP)
433: 
434: ✍️ Please read [my technical blog article about MCP here](https://faafospecialist.substack.com/p/claude-code-solution-to-use-mcp-servers).
435: 
436: ### Pre-requisites
437: 
438: In ClaudeKit, you need to setup the MCP servers in `.claude/.mcp.json` file.
439: 
440: Copy the example file:
441: ```bash
442: mv .claude/.mcp.json.example .claude/.mcp.json
443: ```
444: 
445: Then add your MCP servers, below are some examples:
446: 
447: ### [Context7](https://github.com/upstash/context7)
448: ```json
449: {
450:    "mcpServers": {
451:       "context7": {
452:          "command": "npx",
453:          "args": ["-y", "@upstash/context7-mcp", "--api-key", "YOUR_API_KEY"],
454:       }
455:    }
456: }
457: ```
458: 
459: ### [Human MCP](https://github.com/mrgoonie/human-mcp/)
460: 
461: Human MCP is included in `.claude/.mcp.json.example`, but ClaudeKit disables it by default in `.claude/settings.json` because it requires a Gemini API key. To use it, set `GOOGLE_GEMINI_API_KEY` and remove `human-mcp` from `disabledMcpjsonServers`.
462: 
463: ```json
464: {
465:    "mcpServers": {
466:       "human-mcp": {
467:          "command": "npx",
468:          "args": ["-y", "@goonnguyen/human-mcp@latest"],
469:          "env": { "GOOGLE_GEMINI_API_KEY": "YOUR_API_KEY" }
470:       }
471:    }
472: }
473: ```
474: 
475: ### [Chrome DevTools MCP](https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp)
476: ```json
477: {
478:    "mcpServers": {
479:       "chrome-devtools": {
480:          "command": "npx",
481:          "args": ["-y", "chrome-devtools-mcp@latest"]
482:       }
483:    }
484: }
485: ```
486: 
487: ### Chrome Profile Browser Workflow
488: 
489: Use `ck:chrome-profile` when a task needs the user's actual Google Chrome profile. It opens URLs in the user's real Chrome profile, then the agent selects that tab through Chrome DevTools MCP.
490: 
491: Use it when browser automation needs real cookies, account state, workspaces, tenants, or a specific Chrome profile:
492: 
493: ```bash
494: bash .claude/skills/chrome-profile/scripts/install.sh
495: chrome-profile doctor
496: chrome-profile setup
497: chrome-profile open --json work "https://github.com/your-org/your-repo/pulls"
498: ```
499: 
500: If `doctor` reports no readable bridge, first try a live Chrome DevTools MCP page-list/read probe. The first Chrome DevTools MCP call may trigger Chrome's remote-control approval prompt; after the user approves, retry once before blocking. If both `doctor` and the live probe fail, set up one of these:
501: 
502: 1. Configure `chrome-devtools-mcp` with auto-connect, then restart the agent session and approve Chrome's remote-control prompt if shown.
503: 2. Or attach `chrome-devtools-mcp` to a Chrome remote-debugging endpoint:
504: 
505: ```json
506: {
507:    "mcpServers": {
508:       "chrome-devtools": {
509:          "command": "npx",
510:          "args": ["-y", "chrome-devtools-mcp@latest", "--browserUrl", "http://127.0.0.1:9222"]
511:       }
512:    }
513: }
514: ```
515: 
516: Before choosing a browser tool, decide whether the task needs a specific real Chrome profile: cookies, login/account state, workspace, tenant, or isolated profile key. If it does not, use `ck:agent-browser`, `ck:web-testing`, project-native Playwright/Vitest/Cypress, or Chrome DevTools MCP normal navigation. If it does, do not use MCP `new_page` or raw `navigate_page` as the opening/navigation path. Use `chrome-profile open --json <key> <url>`, then select the page whose URL contains the returned `bind_selector` such as `cdp-open=<token>`. Verify the selected URL also contains `cdp-profile=<key>`. If a live MCP probe proves `doctor` is a false negative, add `--force` only for that launch.
